  "transcript": "# Child Maintenance Service\n\n## How parents actually access the Child Maintenance Service\n> CMS is mainly online and phone-based, provides calculators and information, but lacks sustained, personalised support and struggles with complex incomes.\n\nChair: To start: what support does CMS give parents to set up agreements—both private (family‑based) and statutory?\nDr Samantha Callan OBE: The 2012 system encouraged family‑based arrangements and gives online tools and phone lines, but almost everything is digital and there's little help to sustain agreements as lives change.\nElisabeth Cooper: There is an online calculator that many use first, but it's simplistic and misses complex income—dividends, rental income or directors’ loans often aren't counted in the initial calculation.\nMichelle Counley: CMS is administrative, not advisory. Removing the helpline lost a human touch; decisions feel hostile and rigid, which can antagonise parents from the first contact.\nDr Samantha Callan OBE: There are trauma‑informed training efforts, but online forms are mechanical. Parents need early, face‑to‑face help so small problems don't become big ones.\n\n\n## Errors and unfairness in the calculation\n> The formula and outdated thresholds produce demands many payers cannot afford and leave big arrears; tricky incomes create frequent under‑ or over‑payments.\n\nDr Christine Davies: The current formula is not fit for purpose: it takes a percentage of one parent's income using thresholds set decades ago, producing unaffordable amounts for low earners.\nDr Christine Davies: Because thresholds were fixed in 1998, people who should pay a small flat amount can be assessed hundreds of pounds a month—far beyond their means.\nElisabeth Cooper: Unearned income examples—dividends, rental income, shareholders’ loans—aren't clearly handled by the online system; parents can get absurdly low or misleading assessments.\nDr Christine Davies: The result is rising arrears: NAO highlighted big figures—CMS arrears were hundreds of millions and continue to rise, much of which cannot realistically be repaid.\nProfessor Donald Hirsch: Shared‑care discounts are structurally too small: the formula doesn't reflect real costs like providing a proper home for children who stay overnight.\n\n\n## Conflicts between CMS and the family courts\n> CMS decisions and court orders can clash; CMS jurisdiction can overturn court‑ordered maintenance after 12 months, creating legal and financial uncertainty.\n\nElisabeth Cooper: Courts make final capital and maintenance decisions based on current assumptions, but CMS can review or displace an order after 12 months—causing uncertainty for housing and finances.\nElisabeth Cooper: Practitioners use 'Christmas orders' and other tactics because CMS can later discharge a court order; appeals to tribunals also take years, compounding the problem.\nMichelle Counley: Jurisdiction is badly misunderstood: we've seen people become liable under both CMS and court orders, and CMS has no discretion to apply common‑sense reconciliations.\nElisabeth Cooper: A practical fix would be a CMS information statement for courts: assessment, effective date, basis of income, shared‑care assumptions and pending reviews to help judges decide.\nDr Samantha Callan OBE: This legal‑administrative overlap is sometimes used as part of coercive tactics—multiple forums (court, CMS, tribunals) can be deployed to harass the other parent.\n\n\n## Mediation, family hubs and safeguarding\n> Mediation and local, face‑to‑face family hubs can reduce conflict and spot abuse, but vouchers and online-only CMS services are insufficient safeguards.\n\nMichelle Counley: Mediation can help, but CMS treats parents as separate customers and can't distinguish those who can't pay from those who won't; initial language is often threatening, which hurts trust.\nDr Samantha Callan OBE: Mediation vouchers help but £500 covers at most a session or two; what we need is community‑based pre‑mediation assessment in family hubs so people get early, face‑to‑face advice.\nDr Samantha Callan OBE: Family hubs can spot coercive control and domestic abuse better than phone triage; specialist CMS caseworkers exist, but staff should be embedded locally and trained more broadly.\nLiz Twist: Would embedding CMS staff in multi‑agency settings like MARACs and hubs help spot misuse of the system and give a fuller picture?\nElisabeth Cooper: Yes. Human contact helps explain calculations and evidence, and it would make it harder for a parent to weaponise CMS rules without challenge.\n\n\n## International comparisons and reform suggestions\n> Other countries—Australia, Nordic states—use both parents' incomes, guaranteed payments, or local pre‑action dispute work; the UK needs better data, joined‑up policy and different calculation options.\n\nMari Haapanen: In Finland maintenance is guided case‑by‑case, both parents' incomes are considered and there is guaranteed child maintenance the state pays if the obligated parent cannot.\nDr Christine Davies: Australia divides child costs by age and parents’ incomes and requires a pre‑action dispute resolution step before court—this gives clarity and local support before litigation.\nProfessor Donald Hirsch: We should recognise fixed costs of hosting children—decent housing, equipment, activities—and consider a flat‑rate element alongside proportional payments to protect low‑income parents.\nDr Christine Davies: A core change would be a self‑support allowance: like a personal allowance, payers keep enough to live on, and maintenance applies above that so contributions are affordable.\nJohn Milne: On data and review design: what should a good review measure and how do we avoid repeating poor consultations?\nDr Christine Davies: Success indicators must be affordability, fairness and lower arrears; the review should use CMS administrative data, model interactions with Universal Credit, and test household outcomes.\nProfessor Donald Hirsch: We also need new surveys that capture shared‑care households, not just households as a single unit, because current data hide children who split time between homes.\nChair: Thank you—all panel members have made clear the main fixes: more local human support, clearer court‑CMS information, a rethink of the formula and better data on shared care and poverty.\n",
